body:
`Selection` draws `m` of `n` positions in constant memory but needs `n` up front. Over a stream a caller runs the file twice: one pass to count and one to walk. Today the caller writes both loops, with its own counter and its own position tracking. This is `sail skim`'s streaming arm with the sail-specific parts removed:

```rust
// pass 1: the count Selection needs, which a Reader cannot answer
let mut reader = Reader::new(File::open(path)?, Format::Fasta);
let mut n = 0;
while reader.advance()? { n += 1; }

// pass 2: the draw, with the caller keeping its own position
let mut drawn = Selection::new(n, m, seed).peekable();
let mut reader = Reader::new(File::open(path)?, Format::Fasta);
let mut at = 0;
while reader.advance()? {
    if drawn.peek() == Some(&at) { drawn.next(); take(reader.record()); }
    at += 1;
}
```

Counting, tracking `at` and peeking are the caller's today and need not be. Deciding how to cross a gap is the one part deliberately left to the caller.

## Proposal

```rust
/// How many records `bytes` holds, by one pass over them.
pub fn count(bytes: impl Read, format: Format) -> Result<usize>;

impl<R: Read> Reader<R> {
    /// The global index of the current record.
    pub fn position(&self) -> usize;

    /// Advance until the record at `n` is current. `n` must be at or after
    /// the current position, since a Reader cannot go back.
    pub fn advance_to(&mut self, n: usize) -> Result<bool>;
}
```

Pass 2 becomes:

```rust
let mut reader = Reader::new(File::open(path)?, format);

for n in Selection::new(count, m, seed) {
    if !reader.advance_to(n)? { break; }
    take(reader.record());
}
```

`advance_to` is where a caller with an index would seek instead of scan. That decision stays with the caller.

`count` takes one source and not a chained one. sail-0705fb says why `Read::chain` loses records.

## Open

What `position()` answers before the first `advance`.
